    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d477">

                <pb facs="0623" n="511" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg511"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">701.9493/89: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-05-11T13:00:00+08:00">May 11, 1935—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received May 11—5:58 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>100. Hsu Mo<anchor n="9" xml:id="d477-f1"/> stated to me this morning over the
                    telephone that in a very short time the Chinese Legation in Tokyo and the
                    Japanese Legation in China would be raised to the status of embassies. He asked
                    that this information be treated as confidential and that if the American
                    Government should decide to take similar action with respect to its Legation in
                    China, the Chinese Government would be as pleased as it was with the decision of
                    the Japanese Government.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d477-f1" n="9" xml:id="d477-f1-content">
                <p>Chinese vice Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
